The bug is confirmed. The `startsWith` check on the raw string means `/tmp/work-secret` starts with `/tmp/work`, so it's incorrectly considered inside.

The fix: ensure the resolved root ends with a path separator (or compare exact equality). A portable approach: compare against `path.resolve(root) + path.sep`, but only when the target isn't exactly the root.

Let me write a portable fix:```js
import path from 'node:path';export function resolveWorkspace(root, requested) {
  const resolvedRoot = path.resolve(root);
  const target = path.resolve(resolvedRoot, requested);
  if (target !== resolvedRoot && !target.startsWith(resolvedRoot + path.sep)) {
    throw new Error('outside workspace');
  }
  return target;
}
```

This handles both cases: target equals root (allowed), or target is a proper child starting with `root + sep`. This is portable across platforms since `path.sep` differs (`/` on POSIX, `\` on Windows).

